{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"alias":"cephalic-close-curlew","creation_channel":"cli","description":"## Context / Problem\n\n`compute_validity` (src/rebar/llm/plan_review/attest.py:689-715) has exactly two modes for a\nplan-review attestation: a SCOPED record re-hashes its signed dependency map, and an UNSCOPED\nrecord (empty `deps`) falls back to whole-HEAD freshness — `signed_head != head` ⇒\n`stale-head`, i.e. ANY commit anywhere invalidates it. A ticket that genuinely changes no code\nis unscoped-by-truth and therefore re-reviewed on every unrelated commit.\n\nWith the tri-state from S1 [rebar:1934-63dd-4be9-4c48] available, an attestation can record that its empty\ndependency set is a DECLARED EMPTY SET rather than an unknown one, and skip the fallback.\n\n## Approach\n\nAdd a third scope kind to the signed manifest. `dependency_hashes` already computes the path\nset; a sibling function classifies the scope:\n\n- non-empty deps → `paths` (unchanged);\n- empty deps + the ticket's effective code impact is `none` → `none`;\n- empty deps otherwise → `unscoped` (unchanged).\n\n`build_manifest` (src/rebar/llm/plan_review/manifest.py:309) emits a `code-scope: none` line\nONLY for the `none` kind, so existing manifests are byte-identical for the other two kinds and\nno already-signed attestation changes meaning. A `manifest_code_scope(manifest)` reader\nmirrors the existing `manifest_deps` / `manifest_regver` accessors, and reads from the\nAUTHENTICATED manifest only (ADR 0049) — the plaintext mirror is never consulted, so the line\ncannot be forged onto a signed record.\n\n`compute_validity`'s DEFAULT profile then reads: deps non-empty → re-hash as today; deps empty\nAND `manifest_code_scope == \"none\"` → the code-freshness dimension does not apply, continue to\nthe material/phase/pin checks; deps empty otherwise → the existing whole-HEAD `stale-head`\ncheck. Every other invalidation dimension is untouched.\n\nThe safety interlock is the material fingerprint: `material_fingerprint`\n(src/rebar/llm/plan_review/pass1.py:552) gains `code_impact` and `code_impact_reason` next to\nthe `file_impact` it already hashes. Flipping the declaration in either direction therefore\nyields `stale-material` on the existing check — an author cannot silently convert a\npath-scoped or unscoped attestation into an immune one after signing, and a re-review is\nforced whenever the claim itself changes.\n\n`resign.py`'s drift-refresh path recomputes the manifest through the same builder, so a\n`code-scope: none` record re-signs as one; the explicit assertion is added as a test rather\nthan new branching.\n\n## Scope\n\n- `src/rebar/llm/plan_review/manifest.py` — the scope classifier, the `code-scope:` manifest\n  line in `build_manifest`, and the `manifest_code_scope` reader.\n- `src/rebar/llm/plan_review/attest.py` — the DEFAULT-profile branch in `compute_validity`;\n  export the reader alongside `manifest_deps` in the module's `__all__`.\n- `src/rebar/llm/plan_review/pass1.py` — `material_fingerprint` covers the declaration.\n- `src/rebar/llm/plan_review/__init__.py` — re-export as needed by the existing pattern.\n\nOut of scope: container inheritance (S3), the P9 lint (S3), the review criterion (S4).\n\n## Testing\n\n- `compute_validity` table test over the three scope kinds × (HEAD moved / HEAD unchanged):\n  `paths` re-hashes, `none` stays certified across a moved HEAD, `unscoped` reports\n  `stale-head` — the last of these pins that today's conservative behavior is preserved.\n- A manifest without a `code-scope:` line (a legacy record) keeps whole-HEAD behavior.\n- A tampered plaintext `code-scope: none` on a record whose SIGNED manifest lacks it does not\n  suppress the `stale-head` verdict.\n- `material_fingerprint` differs across undeclared / none / paths for otherwise identical\n  ticket material, and a change to `code_impact_reason` alone also changes it.\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n\n- [ ] An attestation signed for a ticket whose code impact is `none` remains `certified` after\n      an unrelated commit moves HEAD (checked: `compute_validity` test asserting `valid` and\n      verdict `certified`).\n- [ ] An attestation for an UNDECLARED empty-impact ticket still returns verdict `stale-head`\n      after HEAD moves (checked: same test table, unchanged expectation).\n- [ ] `code-scope: none` appears in the signed manifest only for the `none` kind; manifests\n      for `paths` and `unscoped` tickets are byte-identical to pre-change output (checked:\n      golden manifest test).\n- [ ] A `code-scope: none` line present only in a record's plaintext manifest, absent from the\n      authenticated one, does not change validity (checked: tamper test).\n- [ ] Flipping a ticket between `none` and `paths` after signing yields verdict\n      `stale-material` (checked: `compute_validity` test).\n- [ ] `make lint`, `make typecheck`, and the full test suite pass.","id":"749c-7546-6fe3-4d27","parent_id":"94d9-f225-844c-43e5","priority":2,"tags":[],"ticket_type":"story","title":"Attestation scoping: a declared-none code impact signs 'code-scope: none' and skips whole-HEAD drift"},"env_id":"ade0f2ef-51cc-45a2-bb97-c09846cd3df5","event_type":"CREATE","timestamp":1785196785761652001,"uuid":"e4405337-1034-4b40-bb57-90f6a4adf308"}